1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:23,000
There was a time when a very long file was taken as a sign of a skilled developer. Ten
thousand lines in one view controller, worn almost as a badge of honour. That is
emphatically not how code is judged now — and this module is about what replaced it.

2
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The modern measure of quality is this: how quickly could a stranger find the one piece they
need to change? Length isn't skill. Navigability is.

4
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:50,200
Here's how the reference project is organised. Models. View. ViewModel. Domain. Persistence.
CustomViews. Each folder has one job, and you can guess where something lives without being
told.

5
00:00:50,200 --> 00:01:05,800
The failure mode this avoids has a name: Massive View Controller. In plain MVC everything
ends up in the controller — data loading, parsing, business logic, UI updates — and it grows
without limit. That's precisely why MVVM exists.

6
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:22,200
Layered on top of MVVM is Clean Architecture. Three layers. Presentation, which is your
views and view models. Domain, which holds the contracts and use cases and knows nothing
about any framework. And Data, which is models, repositories and API clients.

7
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:34,200
The rule is that dependencies point inward only. The domain layer knows nothing about UIKit,
nothing about URLSession, nothing about where data is stored. That's exactly what makes it
testable.

8
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:37,800
Here's how that's expressed. These are the app's protocols.

9
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:52,200
A protocol is a contract. MovieFetching says: whatever you are, you can give me movies.
BookingManaging says: you can add a booking, cancel one, and tell me which seats are taken.
Neither says anything about how.

10
00:01:52,200 --> 00:02:02,600
Every view model in this app depends on protocols like these — not on a concrete store, not
on a specific API client. Just the contract.

11
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:13,000
That's dependency injection, and it's the most valuable structural idea in this course. The
consumer declares what it needs. Something else decides what gets handed over.

12
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:27,000
And that something else is this file — a composition root. It's the one place in the entire
app where concrete types are chosen. Every screen receives protocols; only this file names
the real implementations.

13
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Here's the payoff. This app reads from local JSON files. To swap that for a real backend,
you'd change the implementations named in this single file. Nothing in any screen or any
view model would need to know it happened.

14
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:56,600
There are three injection patterns in play. Singletons — one shared instance — for things
like the settings store. Protocol injection for anything a feature depends on. And the
factory that assembles it all.

15
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:19,000
A word on singletons, because they get a bad reputation and it's half deserved. The problem
isn't the single instance — it's reaching for it directly all over your code, because then
nothing can be swapped or tested. Here they exist, but confined to this one file. Features

16
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:35,400
One more structural benefit: reusable custom views. This seat map isn't a stock UIKit
control. It's built from vertical rows of horizontal stacks, with deliberate gaps for
aisles, and it takes its layout as data so different cinemas have different shapes.

17
00:03:35,400 --> 00:04:01,000
There's a related problem worth mentioning that only shows up as an app grows: shared
formatting. The same rating appears on the list, the detail screen and the booking summary.
Early on those each formatted it slightly differently, which looked like a data bug but was

18
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:15,800
Here's the reuse in action. The seat map, interactive, letting me pick seats. And the same
component again inside booking detail, read-only. One view, two contexts, configured
differently — which is what the whole architecture is for.

19
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:18,300
Let's recap the key points.

20
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,800
Here is your practice exercise.

21
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:27,200
Module seven is the last technical module: the device capabilities that make an app feel
native.
